{"archetypes":[{"name":"resume-fsm","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds a large resume classifier whose precedence and hydration order are central to safe behavior.","prompt_body":"Investigate the resume-state classification flow in python/ship.py, especially precedence among blocked continuation, checkout mismatch, done, merged, open-pr, and fresh. Check whether helper boundaries preserve the intended ordering and whether any fresh-path fallback can run before branch safety is established. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"state-persistence","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Counter and durable-flag persistence spans run_logs helpers, ship state writes, and repeated invocations.","prompt_body":"Investigate how ITERATION, REBASE_COUNT, FIX_ATTEMPTS, TRANSIENT_RETRIES, durable flags, PR identity, and resume markers are read, hydrated, preserved, and rewritten. Look for paths that zero counters, erase RESUME_PHASE or CALLER_KIND, or write stale argv-derived values during non-fresh resumes. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"github-authority","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Normal-repo routing now depends on gh.pr_view while forked and repo-unavailable paths intentionally skip it.","prompt_body":"Investigate integration with GitHub PR state in python/ship.py, including when gh.pr_view is called, skipped, or treated as authoritative. Pay particular attention to head_ref matching, wrong-head fallback, closed-unmerged handling, and repo_unavailable or forked exemptions. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ci-cap-loop","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The change threads restored counters through monitor, retry, rebase, terminal handback, and cap ordering paths.","prompt_body":"Investigate CI-loop counter semantics after open-pr resume and after monitor decisions. Check off-by-one behavior at the iteration cap, consumed increments for fixer and transient rerun outcomes, and whether merge retry or rebase paths persist the intended values. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"state-injection","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff writes shell-style state files from persisted and contextual values and adds validation gates.","prompt_body":"Investigate shell-state serialization and validation for branch names, repo slugs, PR URLs, merge results, conflict files, and multiline values. Look for injection, path traversal, unsafe fallback, or validation gaps that could corrupt ship-pr-state.sh or influence later shell consumers. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"postmerge-idempotence","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Postmerge, done, and already-merged idempotence are subtle and can falsely mark incomplete work as done.","prompt_body":"Investigate merged and done resume paths plus the main CI success path for postmerge behavior. Check that PHASE=done is written only after successful postmerge, that already-done returns are justified by authoritative state, and that stalled or needs-user postmerge results preserve resumability. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
